From ef1a89f0070afd07f250289a79ca74eab3fac6d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zhenyao Mo <zmo@chromium.org>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 11:36:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [Backport][parts] CVE-2026-113188: Memory corruption in GPU

Manual partial backport of patch originally reviewed on
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8436737:
gpu: Reject separate GL texture mode for WebGPU/Vulkan GL interop

ExternalVkImageBacking normally allocates a single VkImage and imports
it into GL via GL_EXT_memory_object, so both APIs alias one allocation.
When that import is unavailable it falls back to use_separate_gl_texture
mode, which allocates an independent GL texture next to the VkImage and
keeps the two in sync by reading pixels back and re-uploading them on
access, tracked by the latest_content_ bitmask.

UpdateContent() set the destination content bit even when that copy
failed. Because the function short-circuits once the bit is set, a
single failed sync is permanently masked and subsequent readers sample
never-written VkDeviceMemory or GL texture storage.

Create() and CreateFromGMB() already DCHECK that the WebGPU-on-Vulkan-
via-GL-interop configuration never selects that mode, but the DCHECK is
compiled out in release and UseSeparateGLTexture() depends on the shared
image format, which is renderer-controlled. In that configuration GL is
ANGLE on its GL backend, so BGRA_8888 takes the fallback (see
angleproject/4831) even though the drivers interop is enabled on all
support external objects. A well-behaved client never gets there, since
Blink allocates the WebGPU swap texture with the preferred canvas
format and that is forced to RGBA on Linux, but a compromised renderer
can ask for BGRA_8888 directly.

Enforce the invariant rather than harden the sync path.
ExternalVkImageBackingFactory::IsSupported() now rejects any format that
would take the fallback when interop is enabled, and the two DCHECKs
become bail-outs so a caller that skips IsSupported() gets no backing
instead of an incoherent one. Keying the rejection on
UseSeparateGLTexture() rather than on the format also covers
--ignore-gpu-blocklist forcing interop onto a driver without the
external objects extension, where every format takes the fallback.

This is deliberately scoped to the interop configuration:

- Fuchsia runs ANGLE on Vulkan, so UseSeparateGLTexture() is always
  false there and the mode is already unreachable. Fuchsia is also the
  only platform where this factory imports client GMBs, and Flatland
  supports BGRA_8888, so rejecting that format outright would risk
  breaking a shipping path for no security benefit.

- Vulkan on Linux enabled from the command line either comes with
  VulkanFromANGLE, which means ANGLE on Vulkan again and
  AngleVulkanImageBackingFactory taking precedence, or is an unsupported
  configuration. Both are reachable only behind flags and are covered by
  the hardening below.

Also included:

- UpdateContent() now only sets a content bit when the copy succeeded,
  and CopyPixelsFromGLTextureToVkImage() and
  CopyPixelsFromVkImageToGLTexture() return [[nodiscard]] bool to make
  that possible. The rejection above does not cover the flag-only
  configurations that still reach the fallback, so this stays as defense
  in depth.

- CreateFromGMB() initializes latest_content_ to kInVkImage. This is a
  separate bug: an imported buffer's content is in the VkImage, but with
  no content bits set UpdateContent() copies nothing, so a GL read of a
  separate GL texture would observe uninitialized storage regardless of
  any copy failure.

No test changes are needed: the unit test fixture constructs the factory
with interop disabled, so its format coverage is unaffected.

Longer term the fallback should go away entirely, along with
ExternalVkImageBacking being forked for Fuchsia and Vulkan on Linux
requiring AngleVulkan, but that is out of scope here.

This supersedes the approach in crrev.com/c/8421508, which gated
BeginAccess() on the content bits and added a testing-only override to
force use_separate_gl_texture on.

